
{"id":"d8f98f7d-aef8-49cb-ab2d-50b2bfd9581f","object":"chat.completion","created":1785016815,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    all_ids = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\\n    removed = set()\\n    total_required_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_symbols:\\n            total_required_counts[sym] = total_required_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    candidates = []\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['removable'] and n['symbol'] not in required_symbols and n['id'] != 'root':\\n            candidates.append(n)\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    def get_subtree_counts(node_id):\\n        stack = [node_id]\\n        ids = set()\\n        counts = {}\\n        while stack:\\n            cur_id = stack.pop()\\n            if cur_id in removed:\\n                continue\\n            ids.add(cur_id)\\n            cur_node = node_by_id[cur_id]\\n            sym = cur_node['symbol']\\n            if sym in required_symbols:\\n                counts[sym] = counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n            for child_id in children.get(cur_id, []):\\n                stack.append(child_id)\\n        return ids, counts\\n    for c in candidates:\\n        cid = c['id']\\n        if cid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = False\\n        for child_id in children.get(cid, []):\\n            if child_id not in removed:\\n                has_retained_child = True\\n                break\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        sub_ids, sub_counts = get_subtree_counts(cid)\\n        feasible = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            total = total_required_counts.get(sym, 0)\\n            sub = sub_counts.get(sym, 0)\\n            if total - sub <= 0:\\n                feasible = False\\n                break\\n        if feasible:\\n            removed.update(sub_ids)\\n            for sym, cnt in sub_counts.items():\\n                total_required_counts[sym] -= cnt\\n            removed_order.append(cid)\\n    retained = sorted(all_ids - removed)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write solve(case) implementing grammar_guided_node_reduction. The task: given a tree with nodes (id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility), and required_symbols, we need to decide which removable nodes to remove (and potentially their subtrees?) while preserving that all required symbols are still present in the remaining tree. The description: \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\" - typically, we remove nodes that are removable, but we must ensure that after removal, the set of symbols in the remaining nodes still covers required_symbols. Also, we cannot remove a node if it has children that are retained (i.e., if we remove a node, its children must also be removed? Or can children be retained if the node is replaced? The description: \"Never remove a node with retained children.\" That suggests: if a node has any child that is retained (not removed), then that node cannot be removed. So removal of a node implies all its descendants are also removed (or at least not retained). It says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we need to compute which nodes can be removed while preserving required symbols, and also respecting that if a node is removed, its children must be removed (since they are under it). The description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we should process nodes in order of descending depth (deepest first). Use node ID as tie-breaker at equal depth. Then tentatively remove a node if it is removable, and if after removing it (and its subtree), the remaining nodes still have all required symbols. Also, we cannot remove a node that has retained children; but since we process deep to shallow, if we decide to remove a node, then its children will not be retained (because they would have been considered earlier and either removed or retained? Actually, we need to think about the algorithm: we decide which nodes to keep and which to remove. If we process deeper first, we might decide to keep a deep node, but its ancestor might then be considered later. The rule says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has any child that is retained (i.e., not removed), then the parent cannot be removed. So the decision to remove a parent must consider that all its children are also removed. So essentially, removal is a subtree deletion. The algorithm: we need to select a set of nodes to remove (each removable) such that the set of symbols of remaining nodes covers required_symbols, and no removed node has a retained child (i.e., if a node is removed, all its descendants must also be removed). Also, root cannot be removed (root has removable false anyway). Required symbols: nodes with symbol in required_symbols must remain? Actually, required_symbols list specifies symbols that must be present in the tree after reduction. So at least one node with each required symbol must remain. But also nodes themselves might have ids, and we need to retain node ids for audit. The output: removed_node_ids (list of ids of nodes that were removed, in the order they were removed? It says \"actual removal order\" and \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The output schema: removed_node_ids array, retained_node_ids array. Both should contain strings. The ordering: retained_node_ids sorted? The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (probably alphabetically? Or by depth? The task says \"sorted retained IDs\" - typical string sort). removed_node_ids should be in the order of removal (i.e., the order in which we decided to remove them). Since we process deep first, the removal order would be deepest first, ties broken by node ID. So we need to output removed_node_ids in that order.\n\nWe also have utility values, but the description doesn't mention maximizing utility. It says \"grammar-guided reduction\" and \"interestingness oracle\" - the interestingness is preserved if required_symbols remain? That seems to be the only constraint. There's no mention of utility. The task says \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" The interestingness oracle is whether the required symbols are covered? Possibly yes. So we just need to remove as many removable nodes as possible while maintaining that all required symbols are present. Since deeper nodes are considered first, we will greedily try to remove nodes that are removable and not required symbol (the node's symbol might be required, but its node itself might be removable? Actually, nodes have a 'removable' flag, and some nodes with required symbol might be removable? But the rule says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" - that means if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, we cannot remove that node, even if removable flag is true? Or does it mean we cannot remove a node that is required? In Perses, required symbols are symbol types that must be present. So a node with a required symbol must not be removed. But the node itself might have the flag removable true? Possibly but we should still not remove it because it contributes to required symbol. So we must enforce that any node whose symbol is in required_symbols cannot be removed. Also, root cannot be removed. Also, we cannot remove a node that has retained children. Since we are trying to remove as many as possible, the algorithm:\n\n- Build a tree from nodes: each node has children list. Use parent field.\n- Mark all nodes initially as 'kept' (retained).\n- We need to decide which nodes to remove. We'll process nodes in order: sort by depth descending, then by node id ascending (string sort). This gives a deterministic order.\n- For each node in that order:\n   - if node is not removable: skip.\n   - if node.symbol in required_symbols: skip (cannot remove).\n   - if node.id == \"root\" (maybe root not removable anyway): skip.\n   - Check if node has any child that is currently kept (i.e., not removed). If it does, we cannot remove because it would have retained children. So skip.\n   - Tentatively remove the node (and its entire subtree). To check if required symbols are still present, we need to simulate removal. Since we are removing subtrees, we need to know the set of symbols that would be lost. But we also need to ensure that after removal, there is still at least one node with each required symbol. Since we are processing deep first, if we remove a node, its entire subtree goes away. So we must ensure that for each required symbol, there exists at least one node outside the removed subtree that has that symbol. Also, if the node itself has a required symbol, we already skip. So for children, they might have required symbols, but since they are removed, we must ensure that other nodes in the tree (outside the subtree) also have those symbols. Rather than recomputing counts each time, we can precompute the total count of each required symbol in the whole tree. Then, before removing a node, we compute the count of each required symbol in its subtree. If for any required symbol, the subtree count equals the total count (i.e., all instances of that symbol are in this subtree), then removal would lose that symbol, so we cannot remove. Also, we must consider that some symbols may appear multiple times; as long as at least one remains, it's okay.\n   - However, there is a nuance: we are removing subtrees only when the node is removed, and children are automatically removed. So for each node we consider, we need to ensure that after removal, for each required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol not in the removed subtree. Since we are processing deep first, we might remove a node, and later its ancestor might also be considered for removal. But if an ancestor is removed, then the previously removed subtree is already gone, but the ancestor removal would also remove other children. To avoid double counting, we can keep a set of removed nodes. We can compute the subtree set for each node dynamically, but it might be simpler to simulate greedy removal: at each step, check if removal is feasible given current state. We'll maintain a set of retained nodes (initially all). For each candidate node in order, if node is removable and not required and has no retained children (i.e., all children are either already removed or not present?), actually we need to check retained children among its immediate children. Since we process deep first, all children will have been processed before the parent. So if a child was removed, it won't be retained. So we can just check if any child is still retained. If no child is retained, then the node has no retained children (since its entire subtree is either already removed or not present? But what about grandchildren that were retained? If child is retained, then the parent has a retained child and cannot be removed. If child is not retained (either never existed or removed), then the parent can be removed only if all descendants are also removed. But since we process bottom-up, by the time we consider a parent, all its descendants have been decided. So if any descendant was retained, it would show up as a retained child chain. So condition \"no retained children\" is sufficient.\n\n   - To check required symbols: we need to know if removing this node's subtree would eliminate any required symbol entirely. But since we are removing subtrees incrementally, we need to compute the remaining required symbols after removals. We can maintain a counter of remaining occurrences of each required symbol. Initially count all occurrences. When we remove a node (and its subtree), we decrement counts for all symbols in that subtree. But careful: if we remove a node, we should remove the entire subtree (all descendants) regardless of their own states. But if some descendant was already removed earlier, we don't want to double count. So we need to track which nodes are already removed. So algorithm:\n   - Build tree, compute total required symbol counts from all nodes.\n   - Maintain a set 'removed' initially empty.\n   - Sort candidate nodes (all nodes that are removable and not required and not root) by depth descending, then id ascending.\n   - For each node in that sorted list:\n        - If node is already removed (should not happen initially, but if its parent was removed earlier, then its ancestors would be considered later? Actually, if we remove a parent, its children are removed as well, but we will have already processed children earlier because they are deeper. That's fine; we will skip children if they are already removed. So we need to check if node is already in removed set; if so, skip.\n        - Check that node has no retained children: i.e., for each child of node, the child id is not in removed set. If any child is retained, skip (cannot remove).\n        - Compute the set of nodes in the subtree of node (including node and all descendants) that are still retained (i.e., not already removed). Since we process bottom-up, any descendant that is retained will have been considered earlier but not removed; so the subtree will still be intact. But it's possible that some descendants were removed earlier if they were considered and removed. So we need to compute the actual subtree of nodes that are still retained under this node. Actually, if a descendant was removed, then that descendant's subtree is gone, but the node still has that child? The parent-child relationship remains, but the child is removed. So the set of retained nodes in the subtree is the node itself plus all descendants that are not removed. So we need to count symbols from those retained nodes. Then, check if removing this node (and its entire retained subtree) would cause any required symbol count to drop to zero. More precisely, we need to know the current counts of each required symbol in the whole retained tree. Then compute the counts in the retained subtree of node. If for any required symbol, the subtree count equals the total remaining count, then removal would eliminate that symbol, so cannot remove.\n        - If feasible: mark node and all its retained subtree entries as removed. Update the total remaining counts by subtracting the subtree counts. Append node.id to removed_node_ids list (in removal order).\n   - After processing all candidates, the retained nodes are those not in removed set. Collect their ids, sort them, and output along with removed_node_ids.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Build adjacency from parent id to children. Node list contains all nodes. Create a dict id->node.\n- Compute children list for each node.\n- Compute initial counts: dict req_sym -> count (integer) from nodes that are not removed (all initially).\n- Keep a set removed_ids.\n- For subtree retrieval: we can traverse the tree recursively from node, but only for nodes not in removed_ids, and collect ids and count symbols. However, recursion might be deep but depth is small (<1000). We can do a DFS.\n- Since we process in order, we can compute subtree on the fly using recursion. To avoid repeated traversals, we could precompute subtree sets, but they change as we remove nodes. Simpler: for each candidate, we do a DFS to collect retained nodes in its subtree and symbol counts. This is O(N^2) in worst case if many nodes, but N is small (fixtures have 7 nodes). It's fine.\n- After removal, we need to update total counts. We can subtract the subtree counts from total counts.\n\nEdge cases: root might be removable? No. Also, node with required symbol: we never consider for removal. Also, nodes that are removable but have no required symbol, but after removing them, required symbols still present.\n\nWhen checking \"no retained children\": we need to check immediate children only. But if a child was removed, it's okay. So condition: all( child_id in removed_ids for child_id in children_list ) or children_list empty. Actually, if child is retained (not removed), then parent cannot be removed. So condition: any child retained? So if there is at least one child not in removed_ids, then skip. So we need to check that all children are already removed (or there are no children). But careful: if a child is not removed, but its underlying subtree has been partially removed? Actually, if child is retained, it means child itself is still there, regardless of its children. So parent has a retained child. So condition: for each child_id in node.children: if child_id not in removed_ids => retain child, so skip. So we can check: if any child not removed, skip.\n\nBut wait: the rule says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if the node has any child that is retained (i.e., not removed), cannot remove. So condition is: all children are removed (or no children). So we check: if node has at least one child and not all children are removed, skip.\n\nNow, the algorithm as described will produce a removal order (deepest first). That matches the expected output? Let's test with the first fixture case.\n\nFixture case 0:\nnodes:\nroot (depth0, removable false, symbol root)\nn1 (depth1, parent root, removable false, symbol s1) -> required symbol s1\nn2 (depth1, parent root, removable true, symbol s2)\nn3 (depth1, parent root, removable false, symbol s0)\nn4 (depth2, parent n2, removable true, symbol s1) -> required symbol s1\nn5 (depth2, parent n3, removable false, symbol s2)\nn6 (depth2, parent n1, removable true, symbol s0)\n\nrequired_symbols: [\"root\",\"s1\"]\n\nRemovable nodes: n2, n4, n6. But n4 has symbol s1 which is required, so cannot remove n4 (since symbol in required_symbols). n2 has symbol s2, not required. n6 has symbol s0, not required.\n\nSort candidates by depth desc then id: depth2: n4, n6 (id n4 < n6 string? \"n4\" < \"n6\" yes). n4 is required so skip. n6: check children? n6 has no children (depth2, no children listed). So no retained children. Compute subtree of retained nodes under n6: just n6 itself. Its symbol count: s0:1. Total required symbol counts: initially root:1, s1: from n1 and n4 -> 2. After previous removals (none), total s0 count is 2? Wait, total required symbols only root and s1. We care about required symbols. s0 is not required. So removal of n6 does not affect required symbols because it doesn't contain root or s1. So we can remove n6. Remove n6: removed_ids add n6. Update total counts? Not needed for required symbols. removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"].\n\nNext candidate: depth1: n2 (depth1, id n2). Check retained children: n2 has child n4 (not removed). n4 is still retained. So n2 cannot be removed because it has retained child n4. Skip.\n\nNext candidate: after that, there are no other removable nodes that are not required? n2 skipped, n6 removed. So final retained nodes: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. sorted ids: n1,n2,n3,n4,n5,root? String sort: \"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\". That is retained_node_ids. removed_node_ids: [\"n6\"]. Is that correct? But note: n2 has child n4, which is required and cannot be removed. So n2 cannot be removed because it would remove n4? Actually, if n2 were removed, its entire subtree (including n4) would be removed, and that would eliminate the required symbol s1 (since n4 is one of the two occurrences). But n1 also has s1, so it might be okay. However, the rule says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" n2's child n4 is retained (we haven't removed n4), so n2 cannot be removed. So indeed n2 stays. But could we also remove n4? No, because symbol required. So final removal is n6 only. Output removed_node_ids: [\"n6\"], retained sorted. That seems plausible.\n\nNow check second fixture: similar structure, but utility values differ but not used. The same logic yields same removal? n4 is required symbol s1, so cannot remove. n6 removable, not required, no children, so remove. So same removed set.\n\nBut we need to ensure algorithm matches expected behavior. The description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That matches. Also \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" That's done.\n\nPotential issue: what about nodes that are removable and have children that are all removable and all children's subtrees can be removed? Then the parent could be removed as well, but only if after removal of parent, required symbols still present. Since we process deeper first, children will be removed first if possible, then parent can be removed because it then has no retained children. That is correct.\n\nNow, we must output exactly as per schema: {\"removed_node_ids\": [...], \"retained_node_ids\": [...]}.\n\nImplementation in Python:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    # Build id->node dict\n    node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    # Build children lists\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\n    # All node ids set\n    all_ids = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\n    removed = set()\n    # Initial counts of required symbols in whole tree (all nodes)\n    total_required_counts = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        sym = n['symbol']\n        if sym in required_symbols:\n            total_required_counts[sym] = total_required_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    # Candidate nodes: removable, not required, not root, not already removed\n    candidates = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['removable'] and n['symbol'] not in required_symbols and n['id'] != 'root':\n            candidates.append(n)\n    # Sort by depth descending, then id ascending\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\n    removed_order = []\n    # We'll need a helper to get retained subtree nodes and their symbol counts\n    def get_subtree_counts(node_id):\n        # returns (set of ids in subtree that are currently retained, dict symbol->count for required symbols in that subtree)\n        # Use DFS, only include nodes not in removed\n        stack = [node_id]\n        ids = set()\n        counts = {}\n        while stack:\n            cur_id = stack.pop()\n            if cur_id in removed:\n                continue\n            ids.add(cur_id)\n            cur_node = node_by_id[cur_id]\n            sym = cur_node['symbol']\n            if sym in required_symbols:\n                counts[sym] = counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n            # add children\n            for child_id in children.get(cur_id, []):\n                stack.append(child_id)\n        return ids, counts\n\n    # Process candidates\n    for c in candidates:\n        cid = c['id']\n        if cid in removed:\n            continue\n        # Check retained children: any child not removed?\n        has_retained_child = False\n        for child_id in children.get(cid, []):\n            if child_id not in removed:\n                has_retained_child = True\n                break\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Compute subtree (only retained nodes under cid)\n        sub_ids, sub_counts = get_subtree_counts(cid)\n        # Check if removing this subtree would zero out any required symbol\n        feasible = True\n        for sym in required_symbols:\n            total = total_required_counts.get(sym, 0)\n            sub = sub_counts.get(sym, 0)\n            if total - sub <= 0:  # after removal, count <=0 => missing\n                feasible = False\n                break\n        if feasible:\n            # Remove the subtree\n            removed.update(sub_ids)\n            # Update total counts\n            for sym, cnt in sub_counts.items():\n                total_required_counts[sym] -= cnt\n            removed_order.append(cid)\n    # Retained ids: all_ids - removed\n    retained = sorted(all_ids - removed)\n    removed_out = list(removed_order)  # already in order\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_out, \"retained_node_ids\": retained}\n\nCheck: root is not removable, so it's fine. Also, we need to handle nodes with no parent? root has parent null. The children dict for root should have children. Also, nodes like n4 parent n2, etc.\n\nEdge case: what if a node is removable and its symbol is not required, but its subtree contains required symbols? Then removal might zero out a required symbol if that is the only occurrence. Our check handles that.\n\nNow, ensure we return exactly the required keys.\n\nOne more nuance: The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" We already handle root and required symbol. But also \"a node with retained children\" we handle.\n\nThe algorithm should produce correct output.\n\nNow we need to output the Python implementation as a JSON string under \"implementation\". Must be a single JSON object with key \"implementation\". So we produce: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"} with proper escaping.\n\nWe should also import nothing? Standard library only. We use standard dict, set, list. No external imports needed.\n\nPotential issue: The case may have nodes with duplicate ids? Probably not.\n\nNow produce final answer.\n\nBut before finalizing, let's test mentally on second fixture: same structure, removed_order should be [\"n6\"] again. retained sorted: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"].\n\nThird fixture: same.\n\nSo expected output for all three cases should be same pattern.\n\nNow, the task says \"fixture_payload\" shows cases, but we only need to write the function.\n\nWe must also consider that the case may have other fields like \"_case_nonce\" but we ignore.\n\nNow write the code.\n\nI'll produce the JSON with the implementation string.\n\nMake sure to escape newlines and quotes.\n\nWrite the code as a single string with proper indentation.\n\nAlso, the function must not mutate case. We are not mutating case.\n\nReturn value must be JSON-serializable, which it is.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":5902,"total_tokens":8074,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5270},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}